Command Line
************

.[perex]
Everything you run in the terminal: the `check`, `fix` and `baseline` commands, a view of what your configuration really means, explanations of decisions in the terminal or as a document, the catalogue of all decisions, importing the configuration of another tool, every option, output formats for the terminal, CI and other tools, exit codes, the cache and parallel runs.


Commands
========

```shell
dresscode check [paths...]
dresscode fix [paths...]
dresscode baseline [paths...]
dresscode upgrade [paths...]
dresscode config
dresscode explain [decision]
dresscode catalogue
dresscode init
dresscode import <file>
dresscode migrate-suppressions [paths...]
```

- `check` reports violations and writes nothing.
- `fix` fixes what it can and reports the rest. A file is written only when it has changed and the fixed text parses back to the same syntax tree; a file with a syntax error or with a rule that failed stays untouched, and so does a file that an editor or another tool saved in the meantime.
- `baseline` writes the violations a fix leaves into the [baseline |suppressing#baseline] file. Run it on code where `fix` has nothing left to fix.
- `upgrade` rewrites the code to the API of the newer library and PHP versions that the project now requires in `composer.json`. It works even without a configuration, writes in the style the project follows, and offers to install missing upgrade data. It also takes `--modernize`, `--ask-risky` and `--yes`, see [Upgrading Code |upgrade#Options].
- `config` prints what the configuration leads to: the resolved configuration in the shape of the file, with the layer that set each value, and for a decision that does not apply, the reason. See [below |#What the Configuration Means: config].
- `explain` tells you about a decision: what it is for, which values it takes and which one it has in this project; with the name of a section, the same about all its decisions, and without an argument, about the whole configuration. See [below |#Explaining a Decision: explain].
- `catalogue` lists all decisions the installation knows, including those from plugins and the project's own rules, and marks the ones the configuration makes with an asterisk. See [below |#The Catalogue of Decisions: catalogue].
- `init` writes a `dresscode.neon` that fits how the project is written: it measures the indentation, the quotes and the shape of conditions on a sample of files. See [below |#init].
- `import` translates a configuration of PHP CS Fixer or PHP_CodeSniffer into decisions, see [Migrating to DressCode |migration#import].
- `migrate-suppressions` rewrites `phpcs:*` comments to `dresscode:*`, see [the same page |migration#3. Rewrite the Comments].

Paths are files or directories, relative to the current directory. They take precedence over the `paths` key of the configuration, with one exception: a directory that contains some of the paths from `paths` is narrowed to just those. So `dresscode fix .` in the root of the project does not touch anything outside `paths`, and both `check` and `fix` say so right at the start of the output with the words `narrowed to the configured paths`. A directory or file outside `paths` is taken as you gave it. When you give no paths and the configuration lists none, the command ends with the error `No paths given and none configured.`, because it does not want to guess what to check.

`dresscode --help` prints the commands and the common options, `dresscode <command> --help` the options of one command, and `dresscode --version` the version.


Options
=======

These options apply to every command:

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `-c`, `--config <file>` | a configuration file instead of the nearest `dresscode.neon` or `dresscode.php` (or their templates with the `.dist` extension) |
| `--use <name>` | adds a preset or a plugin on top of the configuration; works even without a configuration file; for `init`, the standard and the sets to write; may be given several times, see [below |#Adding a Preset: --use] |
| `--set <path=value>` | gives a decision a value for one run, the value is written in NEON; may be given several times, see [below |#One Decision for One Run: --set and --only] |
| `--severity <name=severity>` | says how the violations of a decision, section, preset or rule are counted, `name=warning` or `name=error`, on top of the [`severity` |configuration#risky-fixes] key; may be given several times |
| `--no-color` | output without colors; the environment variable `NO_COLOR` does the same, and `FORCE_COLOR` forces colors instead |
| `--version`, `--help` | version, help |

These options belong to `check` and `fix`, and where the table says so, also to `baseline`:

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `-f`, `--format <name>` | the output format, see [below |#Output Formats]; also for `baseline` |
| `--diff` | for `check`, shows what `fix` would change; for `fix`, what it changed; only in the `console` format |
| `--only <name>` | narrows the run to the named decision, section, preset, [upgrade package |upgrade#Upgrading a Library You Maintain] or library the data is about, without changing values; also works for `config`, `explain` and `catalogue`, not for `baseline`; may be given several times, see [below |#One Decision for One Run: --set and --only] |
| `--fix-risky`, `--fix-risky=<name>` | makes the [risky fixes |configuration#risky-fixes] of all decisions, or with a name, only of that decision, section, preset, upgrade package or library, not just of those the configuration names in the `fixRisky` key; may be given several times, the name goes after `=`; they are reported either way |
| `--ask-risky` | only for `fix`: fixes as usual, then asks about each remaining risky fix separately, shows its diff and makes those you accept (`y` yes, `n` no, `a` this one and all further ones of that decision, `q` quit); only in an interactive terminal |
| `--max-warnings <n>` | more than `n` [warnings |configuration#risky-fixes] means exit code `1`; without it, warnings do not change the exit code |
| `--stdin <path>` | reads code from standard input as if it were the file at the given path; cannot be combined with paths, see [below |#Standard Input] |
| `--skip-excluded` | skips even a file given by name if the configuration excludes it in `excludePaths`; for hooks and editors that pass file names; also for `baseline` |
| `--no-cache` | processes every file, even one known to be clean; also for `baseline` |
| `--jobs <n>` | the number of worker processes; `1` means running in a single process; also for `baseline` |
| `--strict-rules` | a rule that breaks its contract is an error, not a warning; for developing your own rules; also for `baseline` |

And these belong to `config`, `explain` and `catalogue`:

| option | meaning |
|---|---|
| `--file <path>` | for `config`: what applies to this one file, including the overrides in `overrides` |
| `--preset <name>` | for `config`: prints the preset itself, with what it builds on, instead of the configuration |
| `-f`, `--format <name>` | for `config` and `catalogue`, `console` for reading or `json` as data; for `explain`, `console` or `markdown` as a document |

With `--use`, `--only` and `--fix-risky`, a built-in preset is written short: `--use nette` is the same as `--use dresscode/nette`. A package of the vendor `dresscode` is written short too, `--only upgrade-nette`; a preset from a plugin of another vendor is written in full, see [Standards and Sets |configuration#Standards and Sets]. Decisions and sections are written by their path, `--only blankLines` or `--only blankLines.aroundMethods`, and for a typo, the message offers the closest known name (<code>''Unknown preset `modernizaton`. Did you mean `modernizations`?''</code>).


Adding a Preset: --use
======================

`--use` adds a preset or a plugin for one run, the same thing the `use` key holds otherwise. It takes precedence over all layers, over the configuration and over the overrides:

```shell
dresscode check --use modernizations        # the configuration plus the modernizations set
dresscode check src --use psr12            # someone else's project without a configuration
```

Without a configuration file, `--use` is all the run has. This way you can try DressCode on someone else's project without writing anything into it, see [below |#Checking Someone Else's Project Without a Configuration]. `--use` does not take decisions; that is what `--set` is for, and the message says so: <code>''Option `--use`: expects a preset or a plugin, `controlFlow.elseAfterReturn=forbidden` given; a decision is set by `--set path=value`.''</code>


One Decision for One Run: --set and --only
==========================================

`--set` gives a decision a value as if it stood in the configuration, and `--only` narrows the run to what you name. Both options can be repeated, and together they cover whatever you want to do with a decision just once, without editing the configuration. The value after `=` is written in NEON, so it is exactly what you would write into the file; put a sample with spaces in quotes, so that the shell does not split it:

```shell
dresscode check --set hygiene.is_null=forbidden
dresscode check --set blankLines.aroundMethods=1
dresscode check --set 'spacing.arrowFunction="fn ($x) => $x"'
```

`--only` changes no value. The whole configuration tree is resolved, and `--only` merely selects from it what is reported and fixed. A decision outside the selection stays silent, but its value still applies to everything the selected decisions write: when `--only` runs the rewrite to `match` in a project indented with four spaces, the new block gets four spaces.


Trying a Decision You Don't Have in Your Configuration
------------------------------------------------------

Do you wonder what a decision would do to your code when the configuration does not mention it or your standard leaves it at `keep`? Give it a value and run just that decision:

```shell
dresscode check --set controlFlow.elseAfterReturn=forbidden --only controlFlow.elseAfterReturn --diff
```

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Discount.php
  error  12:5  Useless else, because the branches before it always leave.  controlFlow.elseAfterReturn
--- src/Discount.php
+++ src/Discount.php
@@ -9,8 +9,7 @@
 	{
 		if ($count > 10) {
 			return 0.1;
-		} else {
-			return 0.0;
 		}
+			return 0.0;
 	}
 }

FOUND  1 violation, a fix leaves none in 1 file
\--

The other decisions do not run, and that is why the `return` in the diff stayed indented one level too deep: indentation is the job of `indentation.unit`. `--only` alone, without `--set`, does not run a decision the configuration does not make, because there is no value to run it with; the message says so and suggests `--set`:

/--pre .[terminal]
Error: Option `--only` names decision `controlFlow.elseAfterReturn`, which has no rule that runs here; `--set controlFlow.elseAfterReturn=<value>` decides it for the run.
\--


Introducing a New Decision in One Commit
----------------------------------------

Once you decide to adopt a decision, you want a commit that holds exactly its changes, already formatted by your standard. In a project that is clean according to its configuration, `--set` alone does it:

```shell
dresscode fix --set controlFlow.elseAfterReturn=forbidden
```

The whole configuration runs, plus that one decision, so the other decisions immediately correct whatever its fix moves: the `return` from the example above ends up indented correctly. Everything else was already in order, so the diff contains only what the new decision brought. Then write the decision into the configuration and commit both at once.


Cleaning Up Old Code Step by Step
---------------------------------

A big cleanup is hard to read as a single diff. Split it by intent, one commit for each [set |configuration#Standards and Sets] the configuration uses, or for each section:

```shell
dresscode fix --only cleanup
dresscode fix --only correctness
dresscode fix --only blankLines
dresscode fix
```

Only the `types` set has the same name as a section, so here it is written by its full name, `dresscode/types`, and the section as `types.*`; the bare name could mean either, so DressCode refuses it. The name of a set or a preset in `--only` runs only those of its decisions that the configuration really makes, never more. The name itself decides nothing about any of them, so it cannot overrule what the project left at `keep` on purpose. And nothing is hidden: when you have turned off some decisions of the set in the configuration, the run tells you which ones:

/--pre .[terminal]
Warning: Option `--only` keeps 29 of the 30 rules of preset `dresscode/cleanup` that may run here, without `controlFlow.elseAfterReturn`, `controlFlow.elseifAfterReturn`, which the configuration turns off.
\--

To run a set the configuration does not use, add it with `--use`. `dresscode fix --use modernizations` rewrites the code to a newer PHP and formats the result by your standard right away; `--use modernizations --only modernizations` runs the set alone, and that is also what the message suggests when `--only` names a preset that the configuration does not have in full. A name that would run nothing is an error, not a silently clean run.

`fix --only` fixes only what you name and does not touch the rest, so the final full `fix` still changes something, for example the indentation that the selected decisions shifted. This is on purpose, not unfinished work: each commit carries one kind of change, and the last one is pure formatting. The `baseline` command does not have the `--only` option, because the baseline would forget what the other decisions found.


Checking Someone Else's Project Without a Configuration
-------------------------------------------------------

Without a configuration file, DressCode does not start, because it has no style of its own. `--use` gives it a standard for one run, and nothing is written into the project:

```shell
dresscode check src --use psr12
```

The header of the output then says `Config     none, using psr12`. You can add a set too: `dresscode check src --use perCs --use modernizations` shows, besides the style, what could be written with a newer PHP. When you want a permanent configuration, [`dresscode init` |#init] measures it from the code.


What the Configuration Means: config
====================================

The configuration is a few lines, but they result in hundreds of decisions from several layers. `dresscode config` prints them as the run sees them: in the shape of the configuration file, with the layer that set each value in a comment. It reads the same resolved configuration that the rules and the cache follow, so it cannot claim anything other than what really happens.

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.0.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Target     PHP 8.2 from `composer.json`
Use        dresscode/psr12, dresscode/perCs, dresscode/cleanup, dresscode/types, dresscode/correctness, dresscode/nette, dresscode/modernizations
Targets    php 8.2
Style      a tab, the line ending each file mostly has, lines of up to 140 characters
Names      certain, the namespaces declare no function and no constant
Comments   silence decisions on their line
      ~\bintentionally\s+[!=]=~                               expressions.comparison.loose, mistakes.strictArgument
      ~^//\s*@\s+\S~                                          mistakes.errorSuppression
Decisions  339 of 423 set by a layer, the others asking for nothing

file:
	lineLength: 140                             # dresscode/nette
	strictTypes:
		declaration: required                   # dresscode/nette
		position: sameLine                      # dresscode/nette
...
spacing:
	arrowFunction: 'fn ($x) => $x'              # the configuration
...
classes:
	overridingMethod:
		parentCallOnly: forbidden               # dresscode/cleanup, no effect: types
...
expressions:
	comparison:
		loose: forbidden                        # dresscode/nette
...
mistakes:
	strictArgument: required                    # dresscode/correctness, risky fixes accepted
...
upgrading:
	functions:
		arrayFirstLast: required                # dresscode/modernizations, no effect: php
\--

The `Use` line names the plugins first and then the presets in the order they were applied, including the presets they build on: `nette` builds on `perCs` and the sets `cleanup`, `types` and `correctness`, `perCs` on `psr12`. The `Targets` line says which version of PHP and of libraries the code is written for, and the `Names` line how [unqualified names of functions and constants in namespaces |namespaces] are resolved. Each value shows the layer that set it: a preset, `the configuration`, or an override. A decision that does not apply says why: `no effect: php` when it needs a newer PHP than the project allows, `no effect: types` when it needs types and the configuration has none. A decision whose [risky fixes |configuration#risky-fixes] you have accepted carries `risky fixes accepted`, and a decision that the `severity` key softened to a warning carries `only warns`. When the project uses [upgrade data for libraries |upgrading-libraries] or PHP's own data, a `Packages` line is added, with each package, `php` among them, the version the code must work with, the file the data comes from, and the versions the data would lead to after the lower bound is raised. A version marked `(reported)` lies in the range the constraint already allows, so what it brought is reported without a fix: `upgrading further to 3.3 (reported), 4.0`.

`--file path` shows the same for one file, that is, including the overrides that apply to it, and `--preset nette` one preset alone, with what it builds on. `--format json` gives the output as data for tools: the keys of the environment as they are written in the configuration (`use`, `targets`, `nameResolution`…), in the `decisions` key each decision by its path with its value (`value`), its layer (`layer`), the reason why it does not apply (`inactive`), and whether `--only` selected it (`selected`), and in the `rules` key the classes of the rules with whether they run.


Explaining a Decision: explain
==============================

A decision path in the output is a reference, not an explanation. `dresscode explain <path>` adds its description, the values the decision takes with their meaning, its value in this project with the layer that set it, and what the four standards say:

/--pre .[terminal]
`spacing.arrowFunction`

The space between `fn` and its parenthesis.

By default `keep`.

Takes: `'fn($x) => $x'` (no space before the parenthesis); `'fn ($x) => $x'` (a single space); `keep`

Here `'fn ($x) => $x'`, set by the configuration.

The standards: perCs `'fn($x) => $x'`, psr12 `keep`, nette `'fn($x) => $x'`, symfony `'fn ($x) => $x'`.

Rule `DressCode\Rules\Whitespace\ConstructSpacingRule`, stage Formatting.

See https://dresscode.run/decisions/spacing.arrowFunction
\--

With the name of a section, `dresscode explain blankLines` explains all its decisions. Without an argument, it explains the whole configuration of the project, section by section, and only what some layer decides. With `--format markdown`, it prints the same as a Markdown document to standard output; redirect it to get it into a file:

```shell
dresscode explain --format markdown > docs/coding-style.md
```

The document comes from the same configuration that checks the code. Habits from code review, and everything no decision covers, are not in it.


The Catalogue of Decisions: catalogue
=====================================

`dresscode catalogue` lists all decisions the installation knows, the built-in ones and those from plugins and the project's own rules, each with its description and the names of the rules of other tools that it covers. It marks the ones your configuration makes with an asterisk:

/--pre .[terminal]
* file.finalLineEndings                              Exactly one line ending after the last line; a file ending in HTML is content  (covers single_blank_line_at_eof, PSR2.Files.EndFileNewline)
* file.openingTag                                    The opening tag of PHP  (covers full_opening_tag, Generic.PHP.DisallowShortOpenTag)
* file.lineEnding                                    The line ending of every line, which the code written new takes too; under `keep` that follows the file  (covers line_ending, Generic.Files.LineEndings)
* file.lineLength                                    The widest line, by which what spreads over lines is split  (covers Generic.Files.LineLength, SlevomatCodingStandard.Classes.RequireMultiLineMethodSignature, SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.RequireMultiLineCondition, SlevomatCodingStandard.Files.LineLength)
  file.lineLengthExcept                              What a line too wide leaves as it is: a construct named is not spread over lines for its width, and a line it stands on, one a regular expression matches, or with `strings` one wide only for its string literals, is not reported  (covers Generic.Files.LineLength, SlevomatCodingStandard.Files.LineLength)
...
\--

It is useful when you are looking for what something is called, or where a rule of another tool has gone. With `--format json`, it prints the catalogue as data: for each decision its kind, its range of values, its description, notes, the values of the four standards and the foreign rules it covers. The [Configuration Reference |decisions/@home] on this website is built from the same output.


A Configuration Measured from Your Code: init .[#init]
======================================================

You don't have to write your first configuration: `dresscode init` in the root of the project measures the code you already have and tailors a `dresscode.neon` to fit it. It guesses nothing. For each value of a decision, it runs the rule that would later enforce that value, and every place the rule reports is a place that disagrees with it. So the measuring is done by the same tool that will check the code, and the two can never diverge.

```shell
dresscode init
```

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.0.0
Sample     3 of 4 files in src, tests, 1 generated left out
Standard   perCs, not chosen by measure; the dry runs below count what each would change, not which is nearest, and `--use` writes another
Indent     tab 100% of 3 files
Quotes     single 86%, double 14% of 7 strings
Conditions no conditions
Namespaces no function and no constant declared, resolved for certain
Dry run    perCs       1 of 3 sampled files would change, the one written
           psr12       1 of 3
           nette       3 of 3
           symfony     3 of 3
           laravel     3 of 3

`dresscode.neon` written, made to measure.
\--

The sample is every k-th file of the sorted list, at most 300 files and 2 MB, so that measuring takes seconds even in a large project. Besides the default excluded paths, it leaves out the directories `fixtures`, `Fixtures` and `expected`, files larger than 100 kB, and files whose header says a tool generated them (`@generated`, "do not edit"), because nobody writes any of these by hand. Those of the three directories that exist in the project also go into the `excludePaths` key, so that every later check leaves them out too. The `Sample` line says how many files it left out for their size or as generated. The `Dry run` lines are a dry run of `fix`: they say how many files each of the standards would change, that is, what awaits you at the first real `fix`. They are counted over a hundred files picked evenly from the sample, because each standard means running all its rules; `init` prints the measurements above at once, and the counts for the standards follow later. With a standard given by the `--use` option, there is only one line, for the chosen standard, and it is counted over the whole sample.

Three decisions are measured, each with a different unit, depending on how often it appears in the code:

- **indentation** (`indentation.unit`) by files, because it is a property of the file: `tab`, `4 spaces` or `2 spaces`;
- **quotes** (`literals.quotes`) by strings, because they are decided again for each one;
- **the edges of a multiline condition** (`multiline.edges.condition`) by conditions.

Only what the code says clearly enough is written. A value that at least 70% of the places agree with is written as the value, with its percentage. Where no value reaches the threshold but both together do, a tolerance `[apart, closingApart]` is written for the edges of conditions, meaning "both forms pass", and `keep` for quotes. Where not even that is true, only a comment is written and the standard decides. Above each written decision are its description and the values it takes, so you can read and edit the file without the manual. `init` never writes the `fixRisky` key: consent to fixes that may change the behavior of the code cannot be measured from the code.

```neon
# Written by dresscode init from 3 of the 4 files. A number is the share of the places a decision
# appears in that already agree with its value; what the file does not name, the standard decides.

use: perCs  # not chosen by measure; the other complete standards are psr12, nette, symfony and laravel

# the namespaces of the scope declare no function and no constant, so an unqualified name in a namespace
# is resolved for certain; one declared later is reported until it is listed
nameResolution: certain

paths:
	- src
	- tests

excludePaths:
	- fixtures

fileExtensions:
	- php
	- phpt

indentation:
	# Every line indented by the construct it continues, one level per nesting, the level being this unit; under
	# `keep` a line stays where it is. Values: `tab` (one tab per level); `4 spaces` (four spaces per level);
	# `2 spaces` (two spaces per level); `keep`
	unit: tab  # tab 100% of 3 files

literals:
	# The quotes of a plain string, one without interpolation, without an escape sequence only double quotes know
	# and without a quote of the wanted kind inside. Values: `single` (`'text'` where double quotes gain nothing);
	# `double` (`"text"` for every plain string, the backslashes of a single-quoted one escaped); `keep`
	quotes: single  # single 86%, double 14% of 7 strings
```

Besides the three decisions, `init` also writes what the namespaces declare. Here what matters is not the majority but completeness, so a sample is not enough: `init` goes through all files in the paths, finds the functions and constants declared in namespaces, writes their lists into the `namespaces` key, and with them `nameResolution: certain`. A file that cannot be parsed may declare anything, so in that case `init` does not write the certainty and names the first such file in a comment. The page [Functions and Constants in Namespaces |namespaces] describes what these keys mean. If it finds `phpstan/phpstan` among the installed packages, it also writes [`typeAnalysis: phpstan` |types#enable], with a comment on what types bring.

`init` does not measure the standard. It takes the one you give it with the `--use` option. Without it, in a project formatted by Laravel Pint (one that requires `laravel/pint` or has a `pint.json`), it takes the standard of the Pint preset: `laravel`, `psr12`, `perCs` for `per`, or `symfony`; elsewhere it takes `perCs`. It always adds a comment naming the other standards. Sets given by further `--use` options are written after the standard, so `dresscode init --use nette --use modernizations` writes both names into the `use` key. It takes the paths from the autoload in `composer.json` and adds the directories `src`, `tests`, `test`, `app`, `lib`, `bin`, `cron` and `www*` that really exist in the root; when it finds none, it takes the whole root. If it finds `.phpt` files in them, it also writes the `fileExtensions` key.

Before the file is written, `init` reads it back and resolves it, to check that it gives the same values that were measured. It never overwrites a configuration that already exists: it prints the measurements to standard error, the proposal to standard output, and ends with exit code `3`, so you can look at the proposal or redirect it into a file.


Output Formats
==============

The `--format` option chooses the whole shape of the output, not a detail of it; formats are not combined.

`console` is the default for the terminal: a header with the configuration, the target PHP version and the scope of the check, then the individual files with their violations (status, line and column, message, decision path), and finally a summary. A violation of a decision that the [`severity` |configuration#risky-fixes] key softened has the label `warning` instead of `error`. A risky fix the run was not allowed to make has the label `risky`; when types or `nameResolution` would decide it, the summary counts it as a warning, like the softened violations. For `check`, the summary also says how many violations would be left after `fix`:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php
  error      1  `declare(strict_types=1)` must be on the line of the opening tag.  file.strictTypes.position
  error      3  Expected 1 blank line before the namespace, 2 found.               blankLines.beforeNamespace
  error      6  Expected 2 blank lines before the class, 1 found.                  blankLines.beforeDeclaration
  risky  14:10  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.                  mistakes.strictArgument
                Risky because a strict search no longer finds a value of another type.
  error  20:10  A plain string must be written with single quotes.                 literals.quotes
  error  20:47  A plain string must be written with single quotes.                 literals.quotes

1 risky fix waits, the type is unknown: set `typeAnalysis: phpstan`. See https://dresscode.run/types#enable

FOUND  5 violations, 1 warning, a fix leaves none in 1 file
\--

Above the summary, it counts the risky fixes waiting for permission, grouped by what would decide them, and gives each group advice with a link to the documentation: types (<code>''the type is unknown: set `typeAnalysis: phpstan`''</code>), names of functions in namespaces (`a name may reach a function of the namespace`), or your judgment, where the fix changes behavior by its nature (`changes what the code does`).

When a violation appeared in the file only through the fix of another violation in the same run, the console prints it under the one it follows from, as a `Then also` line with its message and path; if it is on a different line, it says which one (`Then also, on line 7:`). The summary then says how many violations follow from others. In the other formats, it is a violation like any other, with a `derivedFrom` field.

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Box.php
  error  6:11  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                braces.position.class
  error  6:11  Expected a line break after the opening brace.                 braces.position.class
  error  6:33  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                braces.position.function
  error  6:33  Expected a line break after the opening brace.                 braces.position.function
  error  6:36  Expected a line break before the method.                       file.statementsPerLine
               Then also: Expected 2 blank lines before the method, 0 found.  blankLines.aroundMethods
  error  6:68  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                braces.position.function
  error  6:68  Expected a line break after the opening brace.                 braces.position.function
  error  6:87  Expected a line break before the closing brace.                braces.position.function
  error  6:89  Expected a line break before the closing brace.                braces.position.class

FOUND  10 violations, 1 of them following from others, a fix leaves none in 1 file
\--

`fix` prints each file it rewrote with the note `rewritten`, and under it what the fixed text still violates, with lines and columns in the fixed file. The resulting text cannot show which violations the fix removed, so the summary counts the violations found and the ones remaining; `--diff` shows exactly what changed:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php  rewritten
  risky  13:10  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.  mistakes.strictArgument
                Risky because a strict search no longer finds a value of another type.

1 risky fix waits, the type is unknown: set `typeAnalysis: phpstan`. See https://dresscode.run/types#enable

FIXED  5 violations found, none remaining, 1 warning in 1 file
\--

Colors turn off by themselves as soon as the output does not go to a terminal. In a terminal, the decision path of each violation is a link to its description on this website.

`github` is chosen automatically when the run is a step of GitHub Actions, except with `--stdin`, where only an explicit `--format` applies. Each violation is an annotation that shows up right in the diff of the pull request; for `fix`, these are the violations the fix left.

`lines` is for Git hooks and editors: one line per finding in the form compilers write, `path:line:column: severity: message [decision]`, and for a risky fix the run did not make, with the risk after the decision. The column is missing where the finding has none, a line break in the message is a space, and the path is relative to the working directory, absolute outside it, always with forward slashes. For `fix`, these are the findings the fix left; a clean file and a rewritten file with nothing left print nothing. Warnings about the configuration go to standard error in all formats. The page [Editors and IDEs |editors#Findings in the Editor: the lines Format] shows how an editor reads the output.

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Cart.php:13:10: warning: The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`. [mistakes.strictArgument; risk=typeUnknown]
src/Order.php:16:3: error: The line is 144 characters long, more than 140. [file.longLines]
\--

`agent` is for an AI agent that has just edited the file: sentences instead of a table, for a rewritten file a diff of what is already in the file, naming removed imports, and for each remaining violation what to do about it. It leaves out warnings about the configuration. The page [AI Agents |agents] shows an example and how to set it up.

`checkstyle` is XML that Jenkins, the `cs2pr` tool and other CI tools understand; for `fix`, it contains, as with `github`, the violations the fix left.

`json` is for your own tools, see [the next section |#Output for Tools: json].


Output for Tools: json
======================

If you want to plug DressCode into your own tool, such as a code generator, a migration script or a service that checks code from a form, run it from the command line with `--format json` and read the result. The classes that put the run together are internal and may change in a minor version; the shape of this document will not.

```shell
dresscode check --format json src/Cart.php
```

```json
{
    "version": 1,
    "mode": "check",
    "root": "/var/www/shop",
    "files": [
        {
            "path": "src/Cart.php",
            "violations": [
                {
                    "decision": "mistakes.strictArgument",
                    "message": "The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.",
                    "line": 19,
                    "column": 10,
                    "severity": "warning",
                    "fixable": true,
                    "risk": "typeUnknown",
                    "refused": true,
                    "because": "a strict search no longer finds a value of another type",
                    "fingerprint": "2f83e4aa829e87db",
                    "derivedFrom": null
                }
            ],
            "remaining": [
                {
                    "decision": "mistakes.strictArgument",
                    "message": "The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.",
                    "line": 19,
                    "column": 10,
                    "severity": "warning",
                    "fixable": true,
                    "risk": "typeUnknown",
                    "refused": true,
                    "because": "a strict search no longer finds a value of another type",
                    "fingerprint": "2f83e4aa829e87db",
                    "derivedFrom": null
                }
            ],
            "warnings": [],
            "syntaxError": null,
            "failure": null,
            "changed": false,
            "written": false
        }
    ],
    "summary": {
        "files": 1,
        "violations": 1,
        "remaining": 1,
        "refused": 1,
        "changedFiles": 0,
        "syntaxErrors": 0,
        "failures": 0,
        "baselined": 0,
        "exitCode": 0
    },
    "warnings": []
}
```

The header gives the version of the format in `version`, whether it was `check` or `fix` (`mode`), and the root of the project in `root`, which the file paths are relative to. The `files` array contains only files there is something to say about; clean files are not in it. Each file has:

- `violations`: the violations found in the file as it was loaded;
- `remaining`: the violations left after the fix, with lines and columns in the fixed text, that is, exactly what the next check of the fixed file will report;
- `warnings`: warnings about the file, for example about a rule that broke its contract;
- `syntaxError`: the file cannot be parsed, with the message in `message` and the line in `line`, otherwise `null`;
- `failure`: a rule failed in the file or the rules did not agree, with the message in `message` and a link to the documentation in `docs`, otherwise `null`;
- `changed`: the fix changes the text (for `check`, it would change it), and `written`: the file was written.

A violation carries the decision path in `decision`, the message in `message`, the line in `line`, the column in `column` counted from 1, with a tab as one character (`null` for a violation in whitespace or in a comment, and for a token that only the fix wrote), the severity in `severity` (`error` or `warning`), `fixable`, whether a fix exists for the violation (`false` for a finding the rule only reports, and for a decision the project leaves only reported in `reportOnly`), and for a risky fix, `risk` with what would decide it: `typeUnknown` (the type of a value), `nameUncertain` (the name of a function in a namespace), or `behaviorChanges` (the fix changes behavior by its nature); otherwise `null`. `refused` says that the run did not make the risky fix because there was no permission for it, and `because` adds a sentence about what exactly can go wrong at that place, where the risk alone does not say it. `fingerprint` is the fingerprint under which the [baseline |suppressing#baseline] knows the violation, and `derivedFrom` the fingerprint of the violation whose fix this one follows from.

`summary` sums up the run: the number of processed files in `files`, the violations found in `violations`, those remaining in `remaining`, the refused risky fixes in `refused`, the files the fix changes in `changedFiles`, the files with a syntax error in `syntaxErrors`, the files where a rule failed in `failures`, the violations the baseline silenced in `baselined`, and the exit code of the run in `exitCode`. At the end come the `warnings` about the whole run, for example about stale entries of the baseline.

The keys of the structure are camelCase; the data (messages, paths) stays as the tool or you wrote it. Files are printed as they are processed, so even with a large project, you don't wait until the end for the output. Code that is not on disk can be checked or fixed through [standard input |#Standard Input].


Exit Codes
==========

| code | meaning |
|---|---|
| `0` | nothing to report; for `fix` also when the fixed text no longer violates anything; warnings do not change the exit code without the `--max-warnings` threshold |
| `1` | violations remain, a file cannot be parsed, there are more warnings than `--max-warnings` allows, or `baseline` refused to write over code that `fix` would still change |
| `2` | a file failed: a rule threw an exception, the rules got into a loop, or the file changed during the fix |
| `3` | an error on the command line or in the configuration: an unknown option, key or value, a missing configuration; also `init` over an existing configuration |

The failure of one file does not stop the run: the file is reported as failed, nothing is written into it, and the run goes on with the other files. A risky fix that waits for permission and changes the behavior of the code by its nature is a violation like any other, and it keeps the exit code at `1` until someone makes it or writes it into the baseline; one that types or `nameResolution` would decide is a warning.


Cache and Parallel Runs
=======================

DressCode remembers every file that passed clean: its path and a fingerprint of its content, and with them a fingerprint of the configuration that applies to it (including the overrides for its path), of the target PHP version, of the baseline and of the versions of the installed packages. The path is in the key because the same content in two files may get two verdicts, for example because of an override. Next time, a file with the same path, content and configuration is skipped; as soon as any of these things changes, it is processed again. The counts in the summary and the warnings about stale entries of the baseline come out the same with the cache as without it. DressCode remembers content written by `fix` only in a project without a baseline. The cache lives in the system temporary directory, or where `cacheDir` in the configuration points; `--no-cache` bypasses it for one run.

Files the cache does not cover are split among worker processes. The default number matches the number of CPUs, but at most one process per four files, because starting a process has a cost. `--jobs 1` runs without worker processes, which helps when debugging your own rule, and `--jobs 8` pays off on a machine with many cores, where the default estimate would be needlessly low. `baseline` uses worker processes too, so generating it takes no longer than a check.


Standard Input
==============

`--stdin` is the interface for hooks, editors and other tools: the content comes on standard input, and the path says what applies to it (the overrides are matched against this path). `check --stdin` gives a report in any format. `fix --stdin` is a formatter: it prints the whole fixed code to standard output and nothing else, ends with code 0 even when something remains in the code, and refuses the report options (`--format`, `--diff`, `--max-warnings`, `--ask-risky`); [the page about editors |editors#A Formatter over Standard Input] describes what it does on a syntax error and on a failure.

```shell
git show :src/Cart.php | dresscode check --stdin src/Cart.php -f lines
cat src/Cart.php | dresscode fix --stdin src/Cart.php > fixed.php
```

You cannot combine `--stdin` with paths to other files. The cache is not used for standard input. The `excludePaths` key is not evaluated: the code is checked even when the path lies in an excluded part of the project; with `--skip-excluded`, `fix --stdin` returns the code unchanged for such a path, and `check --stdin` reports nothing.


Your Own Command
================

If you package DressCode into your own tool with its own binary, run the whole command line, the `DressCode\Console\Application` class, inside your process, and give it the configuration that applies when the project has none of its own:

```php
use DressCode\Config;
use DressCode\Console\Application;

$application = new Application(defaultConfig: new Config(use: ['nette'], paths: ['src', 'tests']));
exit($application->run($argv));
```

Everything else, that is, the commands, options, formats and parallel runs, stays the same as with `dresscode`, and `run()` returns the same exit codes. It prints the errors that belong to the run to standard error itself: a wrong call and a bad configuration with exit code `3`, a rule failure, rules in a loop, and environment errors of the `RuntimeException` type, for example a file that cannot be written or a crashed worker process, with exit code `2`. Other exceptions, for example `LogicException` or `Error`, mean a bug in the program, and `run()` lets them propagate.
